{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789833882,"nonce":"gfY9GfVs4Gv839D+tonDTy1uEMA=","message":"d0d5ce4: two structural findings on nbody. (1) Instances carried a fields pointer at offset 32 and every compiled field access loaded through it, though the Immix and JIT allocators already place the fields right after the 40-byte header; the only out-of-line case was a class past MAX_ALLOC, which Wren's own 255-field limit rules out, so the limit is enforced at class install and both native tiers address obj+40+8i directly. (2) The LLVM tier's field-kind invariant (check the class byte once instead of each value) was only applied to accessor calls the backend inlined itself; since e1e445b accessors are inlined in place at the MIR level (GuardClassAt + GetField + Move + GuardNumAt), so the invariant never fired and every field read in advance() carried a NaN-box test. Class facts now come from GuardClassAt, resolve through copies, and GetField applies the invariant; advance() went from 22 value tests to 4. NUC under load 2–3: ash/nbody 1.16 → 0.97–1.05, ash/mandelbrot 0.46, bench/nbody 0.113 → 0.107. The default\u003cO3\u003e pipeline and codegen level 3 gain nothing. What remains in the inner loop: the list subscript's kind switch and integer check on an f64 counter, the per-iteration class guard on the loop-invariant receiver (a side exit LLVM cannot hoist), and two safepoint polls.","files":null}]}